 ### Nerfacto Pipeline
 - **Definition:** The default baseline model in Nerfstudio, integrating several advances from recent NeRF literature into a single, efficient pipeline for in-the-wild reconstruction [S3, unofficial].
-- **Step:** Combine a hash-grid encoding for compact and expressive scene representation [S3, unofficial]. Use Mip-NeRF 360 proposal sampling for efficient ray sampling [S3, unofficial]. Apply distortion loss from Mip-NeRF 360 to stabilize convergence [S3, unofficial]. Apply scene contraction to handle unbounded scenes [S3, unofficial]. Apply camera poses refinement [S3, unofficial]. Apply appearance embeddings to account for illumination variations across input images [S3, unofficial].
+- **Steps:** Combine a hash-grid encoding for compact and expressive scene representation. Use Mip-NeRF 360 proposal sampling for efficient ray sampling. Apply distortion loss from Mip-NeRF 360 to stabilize convergence. Apply scene contraction to handle unbounded scenes. Apply camera poses refinement. Apply appearance embeddings to account for illumination variations across input images [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The canonical NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2]. It established the use of coupled MLPs with positional encoding for implicit scene representation. Subsequent developments included NeRFReN for reflections [S1], Neural Body for dynamic humans [S2], NeuMan for monocular human rendering [S1], AR-NeRF for defocus [S1], and SDF-based methods like VolSDF for surface extraction [S1]. The Nerfacto pipeline represents a modern aggregation of these advances [S3, unofficial].
+The canonical NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1][S3, unofficial]. It established the paradigm of using a single MLP to map 6D coordinates to density and color. Subsequent developments addressed specific limitations: NeRFReN (2021) addressed reflections [S1]; Neural Body (2021) addressed dynamic humans [S2]; NeuMan (2021) addressed single-view human rendering [S1]; AR-NeRF (2021) addressed defocus [S1]; and SDF-based methods like VolSDF (2021) addressed surface extraction [S1]. The Nerfacto pipeline (2023) integrated many of these advances into a standard baseline [S3, unofficial].
 
